---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { allLessons } from '../lib/curriculum';

const lessons = await allLessons();
const resourceCount = lessons.reduce((n, l) => n + l.data.resources.length, 0);
const fieldTested = lessons.filter((l) => l.data.fieldStatus === 'field-tested').length;
---

<Base
  title="What this actually is"
  description="An honest account of how Setting Out was made, who made it, what it is worth, and what it definitely is not."
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div
      class="absolute inset-0 -z-10"
      style="background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--sky-low) 0%, var(--paper) 68%);"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[46rem] px-4 sm:px-6 pt-12 pb-8">
      <p class="label">Read this first</p>
      <h1
        class="mt-2 font-display font-900 leading-[1.06]"
        style="font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 1.4rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);"
      >
        What this actually is
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Someone in Perth with a block of sand and no landscaping experience, learning to do it
        properly, and building the course they wanted to exist while they do it. That is the whole
        thing. Everything below is the detail you need in order to judge how much to trust it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[46rem] px-4 sm:px-6 pb-16">
    <!-- ── The disclaimer that matters most ─────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="sign sign-danger">
      <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
        <span class="text-2xl shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">⚠️</span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-display font-700 text-xl leading-tight">
            The person who made this is not a landscaping professional
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            Not a landscaper, not a landscape designer, not a builder, not a horticulturist, not an
            irrigation technician. No trade qualification, no registration, no site experience worth
            the name. Someone learning, in public, on their own property.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            Nothing here has been reviewed by a qualified professional. Where the course states a
            Western Australian threshold or describes structural, electrical, plumbing or drainage
            work, treat it as a well-researched starting point for a conversation with someone who
            is actually qualified — never as the final word.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-garden mt-10">
      <h2>How it was written</h2>
      <p>
        By current frontier AI models — Claude and GPT — working from live web research, with a
        second, independent pass over every lesson that refetched each external link and checked
        that the page still says what the annotation claims. Links that had died or drifted were
        removed rather than left in place. That is how {resourceCount.toLocaleString('en-AU')}
        resources ended up here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be clear about what that process is good at and what it is not. It is good at finding what
        authoritative sources actually say, organising it into a sequence, and being honest about
        where a claim came from. It is <strong>not</strong> a substitute for having built the thing. An
        AI can tell you the first course of a limestone wall must be level. It has never felt a wall
        go out of level at course four.
      </p>

      <h2>Almost none of it has been done yet</h2>
      <p>
        This is the single most important thing on this page. The writing is finished; the doing has
        barely started. Right now <strong>{fieldTested} of {lessons.length} lessons</strong> have been
        carried out on a real Perth block.
      </p>
      <p>
        So there is a <a href="/tracker/">public tracker</a>. Every lesson carries one of three
        states, and the lesson page shows it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Researched</strong> — written and its sources verified, but nobody has done it.
          This is almost everything.
        </li>
        <li><strong>In progress</strong> — being carried out right now.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Field-tested</strong> — actually done, with notes on what was surprising, what it
          cost and what went wrong.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The honest advice: follow the parts that are field-tested. Read the rest, use the
        checklists, but verify anything that costs money or carries risk before you act on it.
      </p>

      <h2>You need a property in Perth</h2>
      <p>
        This is a requirement, not a suggestion. The entire method is that you play both roles —
        the client who wants a garden and the contractor who has to deliver it — on a real block
        with real constraints, real levels, real sand and a real council. Every phase produces a
        document about <em>your</em> site.
      </p>
      <p>
        Without a block you can still read it, and some of it will be interesting. But the course
        will not work, because the work is the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also specifically <strong>Perth</strong>. Bassendean and Spearwood sands, a permanent
        sprinkler roster, a total winter ban, retaining wall thresholds unlike any other state.
        Someone in Melbourne would be misled by a great deal of this.
      </p>

      <h2>It will sometimes send you elsewhere, including to paid things</h2>
      <p>
        Where a paid course, a book, an Australian Standard or a professional assessment is
        genuinely the best option, the course says so. It is not trying to be the only thing you
        read — it is trying to be an honest map of what is worth your time and money, with the best
        free alternative named beside every paid one.
      </p>
      <p>
        It will also tell you to get your work professionally reviewed, and it means it. Retaining
        walls, drainage design, anything structural, anything electrical, plumbing, gas or pool
        barriers — get a qualified person to look at it. In Western Australia several of those are
        not optional, and the course flags them with a warning sign you cannot scroll past.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it exists at all</h2>
      <p>
        Two honest reasons. Landscaping looks genuinely absorbing as a craft — at least when you are
        doing it for yourself rather than to a deadline for someone else — and a well-made garden
        adds real value to a house. Learning the trade properly and improving the property are the
        same activity here.
      </p>
      <p>
        But there is a second reason, and it is the one that made this worth building rather than
        just reading a few books.
      </p>

      <h2>The experiment: course first, platform second</h2>
      <p>
        The person building this spent years at Moodle HQ and then four more freelancing on Moodle.
        So the usual shape of this problem is very familiar: you take a learning platform, you
        author a course inside it, and lately you bolt AI features on top. Platform → course → AI.
      </p>
      <p>
        This went the other way round. <strong>AI → course → platform.</strong> Frontier models were
        used to build deeply specific content for exactly <strong>one</strong> learner, in one city,
        on one kind of soil, under one state's regulations — and only then was a small
        learning-platform experience built around that content.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point is that a course this specific could never be commissioned. Nobody is going to
        write ninety-one lessons on landscaping the Swan Coastal Plain for an audience of one. If
        that turns out to work, the interesting question is not "how do we add AI to an LMS" but
        "what does a course look like when it is made for a single person and their actual
        circumstances". That is being tested here as much as the gardening is.
      </p>

      <h2>Not commercial, and not trying to be</h2>
      <p>
        There is nothing to buy, no advertising, no email list, no analytics on your account. The
        course content is CC BY-SA, the code is
        <a href="https://github.com/mouneyrac/setting-out">MIT and public</a>. Where a paid course or
        book is genuinely the best next step, the course says so and gains nothing from it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If it turns out to work, the hope is that other people with a Perth block do it too — and
        that it becomes something closer to a small community walking the same path than a thing
        one person made alone. The tracker is public precisely so anyone joining can see how far
        ahead the path has actually been walked.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you <em>are</em> a WA landscaping professional and something here is wrong, please
        <a href="https://github.com/mouneyrac/setting-out/issues">open an issue</a>. Being corrected
        is the fastest way this gets better, and there is no ego invested in being right.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-10">
      <a href="/tracker/" class="btn btn-primary">See what has actually been done</a>
      <a href="/curriculum/" class="btn btn-ghost">The eleven phases</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
